    <node id="CVE-2016-10538">
      <data key="d0">CVE</data>
      <data key="d1">javascript</data>
      <data key="d2">[security] Insecure usage of temporary files.</data>
      <data key="d3">The current code makes use of predictable filenames, in a way that causes a security issue.

I reported this to Debian last year:
- https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=809252

It was recently highlighted by the nodesecurity people (six months later!):
- https://nodesecurity.io/advisories/95

Suggested fix:
- Avoid using predictable filenames in world-writable directories.
  - Using `~/.app.pid` would be better than `/tmp` for example.
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="node-js-libs/cli#81">
      <data key="d0">Issue</data>
      <data key="d2">[security] Insecure usage of temporary files.</data>
      <data key="d3">The current code makes use of predictable filenames, in a way that causes a security issue.

I reported this to Debian last year:
- https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=809252

It was recently highlighted by the nodesecurity people (six months later!):
- https://nodesecurity.io/advisories/95

Suggested fix:
- Avoid using predictable filenames in world-writable directories.
  - Using `~/.app.pid` would be better than `/tmp` for example.
</data>
      <data key="d4">[]</data>
      <data key="d1">javascript</data>
