    <node id="CVE-2019-14459">
      <data key="d0">CVE</data>
      <data key="d1">c</data>
      <data key="d2">Integer overflow in Process_ipfix_template_withdraw (ipfix.c) </data>
      <data key="d3">Hi! 

I found an integer overflow at function `Process_ipfix_template_withdraw` that can be abused in order to crash the process remotely (denial of service):

```
Program received signal SIGSEGV (current pc: 0x55b9f281a75f)


   f 0     55b9f281a75f Process_ipfix_template_withdraw+41
   f 1     55b9f281a23f Process_ipfix_templates+100
   f 2     55b9f281c337 Process_IPFIX+525
   f 3     55b9f2803d4f run+3406
   f 4     55b9f2805530 main+5659
   f 5     7f5d713112e1 __libc_start_main+241
```

The function uses `size_left` as uint32_t which is an unsigned integer (only can holds values between 0 and 2^32), so if we have a `size_left` with value 1, when the substraction at line 1429 is done (`size_left -= 4;`), it will overflow and become 4294967293 (0xfffffffd). As this is a value higher than "4", the size check made at line 1443 (`if ( size_left &lt; 4 ){(...) size_left = 0; (...)}`) will be bypassed. At this point we have a huge loop (`while ( size_left )`) where the pointer `DataPtr` will be increased by 4 in each iteration until it reaches an invalid memory address and segfaults.

I hope this information can be useful.

Best regards,
Juan Manuel Fernandez</data>
    </node>
    <node id="phaag/nfdump#171">
      <data key="d0">Issue</data>
      <data key="d2">Integer overflow in Process_ipfix_template_withdraw (ipfix.c) </data>
      <data key="d3">Hi! 

I found an integer overflow at function `Process_ipfix_template_withdraw` that can be abused in order to crash the process remotely (denial of service):

```
Program received signal SIGSEGV (current pc: 0x55b9f281a75f)


   f 0     55b9f281a75f Process_ipfix_template_withdraw+41
   f 1     55b9f281a23f Process_ipfix_templates+100
   f 2     55b9f281c337 Process_IPFIX+525
   f 3     55b9f2803d4f run+3406
   f 4     55b9f2805530 main+5659
   f 5     7f5d713112e1 __libc_start_main+241
```

The function uses `size_left` as uint32_t which is an unsigned integer (only can holds values between 0 and 2^32), so if we have a `size_left` with value 1, when the substraction at line 1429 is done (`size_left -= 4;`), it will overflow and become 4294967293 (0xfffffffd). As this is a value higher than "4", the size check made at line 1443 (`if ( size_left &lt; 4 ){(...) size_left = 0; (...)}`) will be bypassed. At this point we have a huge loop (`while ( size_left )`) where the pointer `DataPtr` will be increased by 4 in each iteration until it reaches an invalid memory address and segfaults.

I hope this information can be useful.

Best regards,
Juan Manuel Fernandez</data>
      <data key="d4">[]</data>
      <data key="d1">c</data>
    </node>
